    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 5yr CAGR (4.0%) is 33% below EPS 5yr CAGR (6.0%): Cisco returns more than 100% of net income via ~$6B buybacks + ~$6.5B dividends a year, so book value understates the compounding. Revenue 5yr CAGR (4.9%) selected instead.",
    "notes": "FCF CAGRs are on the owner-FCF series (reported FCF - SBC - equity-award withholding taxes): FY2021 $12,365M, FY2023 $16,087M, FY2026 $7,064M. The owner-FCF CAGR is far below the reported-FCF CAGR because SBC + withholding rose from $2.4B (FY21) to $5.7B (FY26): an increasing share of reported cash flow is paid in stock. Both endpoints are also distorted by tax timing: FY2023 FCF was inflated by the IRS disaster-relief deferral of California tax payments into FY2024, and FY2026 carries the final $2.3B TCJA transition-tax payment. Ex-transition-tax, the FY26 owner FCF is ~$9.4B (3yr CAGR -16.5%, 5yr -5.4%). Revenue includes Splunk from Q4 FY2024 (acquired March 2024, ~$28B), so FY24-26 revenue growth is partly acquired."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Cisco converts its record order book into FY27 guidance (revenue $72.2-73.4B, non-GAAP EPS $5.05-5.11), with hyperscaler AI infrastructure revenue rising from ~$4B to ~$7.5B. After FY27 the supercycle normalises rather than reverses: enterprise campus refresh, security (Splunk, Hypershield) and observability hold the business at 4-6% growth fading to 3%, and margins stay at the FY26-27 level because AI hardware mix dilution offsets software mix gains. SBC plus withholding stays ~7-8% of revenue, funded by ~$6B/yr buybacks.",
    "bull": "The AI networking supercycle is durable and Cisco wins share in it: hyperscaler orders (already $9.3B in FY26) keep compounding past FY27, Silicon One and optics win 800G/1.6T scale-out and sovereign/neocloud AI builds, and enterprises re-architect campus and data-center networks for AI inference. Splunk and security become a larger, higher-margin recurring base (56% of revenue recurring, RPO $46.7B), lifting EBITDA margin ~2pp over five years. Revenue grows high-single-digit through FY30.",
    "bear": "The FY26-27 surge proves to be a hyperscaler capex cycle, not a structural step-up. Arista (already ahead in 100G-800G data-center switching) and white-box/Nvidia Spectrum-X take the next generation of AI back-end networking, a handful of concentrated webscale customers pause orders and FY28 revenue falls ~4%. Lower-margin AI hardware, tariffs and restructuring disruption (~4,000 job cuts in May 2026) compress margins ~2.5pp, and the legacy switching/routing/collaboration core reverts to ~2% growth.",
    "_source": "Bull/bear axes from CSCO_Analysis.json (2026-09) bull_case / bear_case / risks; guidance from the Q4 FY2026 earnings release (8-K exhibit 99.1, 2026-08-12)."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Cisco is valued on a strict owner-FCF basis: SBC and the cash taxes Cisco pays on net-share settlement are both treated as real costs (together ~9% of FY26 revenue), and the share count is held flat because ~$6B/yr of buybacks keeps it flat. FY27 is anchored on management's guidance; outer years fade to mature-networking growth. On this basis the market price sits above the house value and much of the way toward (or beyond) the street basis that treats SBC as non-cash: the current price is paying for the AI networking supercycle continuing beyond FY27.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Hyperscaler AI order concentration: FY27 guidance includes ~$7.5B hyperscaler AI revenue that could pause quickly",
      "Share loss to Arista / white-box / Nvidia Spectrum-X in high-speed AI data-center switching",
      "SBC + withholding treatment: ~$5.7B/yr; the house vs street gap is ~$25-30/share",
      "Gross-margin dilution from lower-margin AI hardware mix and tariffs",
      "Owner-FCF base distortion: FY26 includes the final $2.3B TCJA transition-tax payment and a working-capital build; FY23 was inflated by a tax deferral"
    ]
  },
